ddbcbb2008" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050511 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049053 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050511 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049083 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050511 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049137 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050511 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049619 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050511 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050865 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050511 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186068731 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050511 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186068732 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050511 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186068733 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050511 #edb/ref 1031 "Solving Exponential Equations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050511 #edb/ref 1033 391 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050511 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "4f6afc08-f23d-50b8-a5e5-a6052d6e4308" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050865 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049806 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050865 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186064150 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050865 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186064151 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050865 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186064275 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050865 #edb/ref 1031 "Removing Point Discontinuities" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050865 #edb/ref 1033 341 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050865 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "6c838d5e-1457-5ed8-957b-544017d2ddfe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064033 #edb/ref 1069 "e-258" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064033 #edb/ref 1071 "The function $g(x)$ is given by\n${\\sqrt{bx + 3},\\begin{vmatrix}x \\ne 2, \\\\ 5,\\end{vmatrix}x = 2$.\nIf $g(x)$ is continuous at $x=2$, what is the value $b$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064033 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e6c4219b-1955-5fa0-a362-6cc273510d88" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064033 #edb/ref 1074 "From the definition of $g(x)$,\n\n$$\nlim_(x → 2)g(x) = lim_(x → 2)(\\sqrt{bx + 3}) = \\sqrt{2b + 3}\n$$\n\nIn order for $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2$, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 2)g(x) &= g(2) \\\\\n\\sqrt{2b + 3} &= 5 \\\\\n2b + 3 &= 25 \\\\\n2b &= 22 \\\\\nb &= 11\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064034 #edb/ref 1069 "e-259" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064034 #edb/ref 1071 "The function $h(x)$ is given by\n${\\log_{3} (x + c),\\begin{vmatrix}x \\ne 0, \\\\ 2,\\end{vmatrix}x = 0$.\n\nGiven that $h(x)$ is continuous at $x=0$, what is the value of $c$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064034 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b411bf9f-f944-505d-94dc-13df743545dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064034 #edb/ref 1074 "From the definition of $h(x)$, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 0)h(x) &= lim_(x → 0)\\log_{3} (x + c) \\\\\n&= \\log_{3} (0 + c) \\\\\n&= \\log_{3} (c)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $h(x)$ to be continuous at $x=0$, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 0)h(x) &= h(0) \\\\\n\\log_{3} (c) &= 2 \\\\\nc &= 3^{2} \\\\\nc &= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting $c=9$ removes the point discontinuity." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064118 #edb/ref 1069 "e-6320" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064118 #edb/ref 1071 "The function $f(x)$ is defined for every real number $x$ and is given by\n${x^{2} + 2kx + 1,\\begin{vmatrix}x \\ne 1, \\\\ 4,\\end{vmatrix}x = 1$.\nIf $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1$, what is the value of $k$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064118 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5443849c-193c-58a9-ae6c-1b859bbd1d4e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064118 #edb/ref 1074 "From the definition of $f(x)$,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 1)f(x) &= lim_(x → 1)(x^{2} + 2kx + 1) \\\\\n&= 1^{2} + 2k(1) + 1 \\\\\n&= 2 + 2k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=1$, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 1)f(x) &= f(1) \\\\\n2 + 2k &= 4 \\\\\n2k &= 2 \\\\\nk &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting $k=1$ removes the point discontinuity." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064150 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048784 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064150 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186064819 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064150 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186064822 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064150 #edb/ref 1039 "Removing a Point Discontinuity in a Piecewise Radical Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064150 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186064033 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064150 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "40d01201-07ca-5083-a7f8-095be503df27" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064151 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048741 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064151 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049944 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064151 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050511 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064151 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186064809 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064151 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186064823 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064151 #edb/ref 1039 "Removing a Point Discontinuity in a Piecewise Exponential or Logarithmic Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064151 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186064034 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064151 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "50eadb82-ffa5-5ee5-b878-1c04e41fba7e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064275 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048614 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064275 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049593 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064275 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186064755 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064275 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186064808 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064275 #edb/ref 1039 "Removing a Point Discontinuity in a Piecewise Polynomial Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064275 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186064118 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064275 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "c931e4a8-ad1d-5a5a-8f8f-f3901efda408" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064432 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186064976 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064432 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186064977 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064432 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186064978 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064432 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186065161 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064432 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064432 #edb/ref 1045 "Removing Point Discontinuities" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064432 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186064976 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064432 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050865 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064432 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "0daebd08-5b3a-5348-97e2-30af6666d226" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064976 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065161 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064976 #edb/ref 1061 1003 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064976 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "14b7beb0-d51f-5caa-8ccf-0fcaf66bc2a2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064976 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186065391 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064977 #edb/ref 1061 1004 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064977 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2c19e04f-b050-5aa6-b53b-b03d3c0a2722" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064977 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064150 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064978 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186064977 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064978 #edb/ref 1061 1008 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064978 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ad697e66-f2f7-5931-bcd6-af97dd02e4c7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064978 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064151 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065161 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186064978 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065161 #edb/ref 1061 23759 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065161 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5ce3c605-d5cc-5d33-99f5-00d65adf3013" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065161 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064275 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065391 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the function $f(x)$ below.\n\n$$\n{3x + a,\\begin{vmatrix}x \\ne 4, \\\\ 13,\\end{vmatrix}x = 4\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ seems to have a point discontinuity at $x=4$. However, by selecting a particular value for $a$, we can remove the point discontinuity and make the function continuous.\n\nWe can find this value of $a$ by forcing the limit of the function to be equal to the value of the function at $x=4$:\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 4} f(x) = f(4)\n$$\n\nRemember that $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 4} f(x)$ represents the value that $f(x)$ approaches as $x$ gets closer and closer, but not equal, to $4$. So, when evaluating $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 4} f(x)$, we need to use the function definition for\n\n$$\nx \\neq 4\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 4)f(x) &= lim_(x → 4)(3x + a) \\\\\n&= 3(4) + a \\\\\n&= 12 + a\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $x=4$, the function is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=13\n$$\n\nSo\n\n$$\nf(4)=13\n$$\n\nand we can solve the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 4)f(x) &= f(4) \\\\\n12 + a &= 13 \\\\\na &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to make $f(x)$ continuous at $x=4$, we need to choose $a=1$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065391 #edb/ref 1065 535 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065391 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ec3c5bfd-8781-5ecb-9e08-fe301193f6ea" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065391 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
